{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261026T183718Z-59f3284c", "ts": "2026-10-26T18:40:25Z", "actor": {"worktree": "samsung-frame-art-loader", "plugin": "3.8.0-dev", "branch": "feature/wave-4f-scenes"}, "body": {"base_commit": "56f458bf96ca8389f038dfee7200135ebbfd9835", "base_tree": "aac6a15f9a93c58ef5762b1281f96a5ae1d9ff52", "head_tree": "944b90062ba761f6710d3de94cbe0151df1a5466", "head_commit": null, "dispatch_commit": "56f458bf96ca8389f038dfee7200135ebbfd9835", "mode": "final (full review, ready for push)", "mode_chosen_by": "plan-override: final", "stage": "inner", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["arrt/src/arrt/persistence/records.py", "arrt/src/arrt/persistence/sqlite.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/manifest/v2.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/store.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_scenes.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-wave-4f-scenes.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", "arrt/src/arrt/persistence/records.py", "arrt/src/arrt/persistence/sqlite.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/manifest/v2.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/store.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_scenes.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-wave-4f-scenes.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings Cross-Check: two rules bear, one regression found", "recommendation": "Scope: none (mandated cross-check). Read core.md and surface.md (`learnings-files --for-diff`). The change regresses against one rule. 'When code is added, moved or retired, ask which guards were scoped to the old shape...': the heartbeat's bulkhead still covers only `keep_showing`, and the new `_settle_scenes` step runs outside it (see the heartbeat observation). It conforms to these: 'When a comment names a symbol as the source of truth for a set, parametrise the test over that symbol' (test_scenes derives the `_publish_feed` callers from source and parametrises over them); 'When you write or cite exactly one, always or never, state what the store or guard actually enforces' (`draft_scene` says the store would hold more than one draft, and the pin PK enforces one wall per scene). 'When a result gains a collection, write down what bounds it' bears on the scenes tables (see the growth observation). No rules were added or changed in this cycle. No rule-unenforced finding.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog Reconciliation: nothing resolved; #216 should learn about the two new tables (C-B3)", "recommendation": "Scope: none. `affecting` the changed files returned #143 and #376, and this change resolves neither. No open item matches 'scene'. C-B3: the diff touches the programming area, which has open items #216, #307 and #374. The plan's tradeoffs already say 'that split moves two more tables' about #216, but #216 itself was not updated. Add `scenes` and `scene_pins` to #216's body via `/prawduct:backlog update`, so whoever splits the store moves them. Cited ids #216 and #309 both resolve, so there is no dangling C-B4 id.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/persistence/sqlite.py"], "fid": "R-2"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 0, "note": 2}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Feed's new rule-5 refusal and widened rule-1 check have no test of their own", "recommendation": "Instance. Feed.__post_init__ now raises 'Scene ... ends before it starts' and checks scene and staging ids against `works` ('The feed names works it does not carry'), but no test constructs a Feed that trips either one. No test covered the old schedule-only message either. No service path can reach these today: apply sets until = now + at least 1 minute, and _control_layer filters out expired scenes. That is why this is an observation and not the inner 'changed behavior with no test' BLOCKING. Add two small unit cases in test_scenes.py or test_major_2_feed.py: a SceneEntry with until <= starts_at, and a staging id missing from works, each asserting the ValueError. Per the learning 'When a test, lint or check is new or rewritten, watch it fail once', see each one fail against a re-break.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/manifest/v2.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_scenes.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Two journal events and the wrong-state refusals have no test", "recommendation": "Instance. The plan's Surfaces name one log event per act. Only `scene.expired` is asserted (test_a_heartbeat_writes_down_that_a_preview_ran_out). `scene.applied` (apply_scene) and `scene.released` (release_scene, and _end_live for a replaced scene) are never asserted, so a wrong `wall_ids` or event name would stay green. Of the _SCENE_NOT refusals, only release-of-a-draft is tested. Pin, unpin and discard on a live or ended scene, and apply on a live one, are not. Add caplog assertions keyed on `event` (not position) for applied and released, including the replaced-scene path. Add one parametrised case for acting on a scene that is not a draft.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_scenes.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Withdraw and reconcile branches for control-only works write a misleading journal line", "recommendation": "Class, bounded to the two new control-only branches. (1) _withdraw_v2 now patches and logs 'took X off the feed' whenever X is only in `carried`. Through leave_theme, a work that leaves its theme is still playable and not kept off. When that work is the wall's live scene pin or a staged work, _control_layer puts it straight back: the feed is rewritten, the work stays, and the journal says it was taken off. The same false line appears when the work is both scheduled and pinned: the rebuild drops it from the schedule and the control layer re-adds it. (2) When the only gone work is control-only, _reconcile_v2 takes the `fresh or gone` branch. It logs 'pointed the feed at the current master or colour of ' with `fresh` empty. Make each branch log what the written feed actually lost: compare `carried` before and after, or log the gone-only case separately. Add a leave_theme case with a pinned work that asserts the scene survives and the line is not written. Behaviour on the wall is correct in both cases; only the record is wrong.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "Done-when 6 (mutation sweep over the control layer and as_document's two keys) has no recorded result", "recommendation": "Instance. Chunk 01's Done-when item 6 asks for a sweep with each survivor explained. Nothing in the plan, the change log or the staged diff records one. The chunk is not ticked, so this is owed, not dropped. Run it before ticking 01 (`cd arrt && uv run python tools/mutation_sweep.py ...`) and record the mutations and verdicts. Per the learning 'When a mutation sweep reports, confirm each mutation changed behaviour, matched once, and the run collected tests'. Or descope the item explicitly.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/manifest/v2.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Published.scene and Published.staging are parsed back but nothing reads them, and parsing them can only cost the feed", "recommendation": "Class: dead read-back path. read_published now builds a SceneEntry and a staging tuple from the published document, but no code in arrt/src reads Published.scene or Published.staging (grep: the only `.scene`/`.staging` reads are on Feed in _publish_feed and Feed.named, plus SceneView in tests). The plan's own decision is that the control layer is 'never read back from the feed'. Parsing it is still not harmless: a scene object missing `id`, `from` or `until` raises KeyError inside the same try, so read_published returns None for the whole feed. Callers then treat the wall as having no feed: pin_scene refuses the wall, and _patch_walls skips it with a warning. Drop the two fields and their parsing. Keep control_works, which is used, because carried needs it. Or, if a later chunk needs the published scene, add the read in that chunk with a consumer.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/manifest/v2.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Published.works keeps the document's key name but now means only the schedule's works, so every consumer has to choose works or carried, and some chose without being asked", "recommendation": "Class. The change from 'every entry the document carries' to 'the schedule's entries' is right for the rebuild paths. But the attribute still mirrors the JSON key `works`, which carries both. Readers that did not change now silently see the narrower set. For example, judge_sizes (`feed.works.items()`) no longer judges a pinned or staged work for too-small, so a curator can pin a work too small for the wall and get no too-small warning. The ~17 read_published consumers in display.py are the search scope. Construction: rename the field to say what it holds (e.g. `scheduled`), so each consumer's choice is visible in the code. Then decide per consumer, starting with judge_sizes, whether the control layer belongs in it.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/manifest/v2.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Ending a scene is written three times, and 'latest applied wins' twice, with no single owner of the transition", "recommendation": "Instance. The transition to ENDED appears separately in release_scene (ended_at=now), _end_live (ended_at=now, logs as scene.released) and _settle_scenes (ended_at=until, logs as scene.expired). Each one replaces the status, sets ended_at, lists the pins for the walls and logs. _scene_view also builds a fourth, unstored ENDED (ended_at=until) for display. The rule picking one of several live scenes (`max(key=starts_at or created_at)`) is duplicated in live_scene and _control_layer. The derived status itself has one source of truth (Scene.status_at), so this is duplication rather than an unmodelled state problem. Two changes would let chunk 02's History events attach in one place rather than three: one `_end_scene(scene, at, event)` helper returning the walls, and one 'the live scene for these candidates' helper.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The draft read paths assume one draft, but _control_layer stages every draft", "recommendation": "Instance; borderline (NOTE-level). draft_scene and scene_draft show drafts[-1]. _control_layer stages the pins of every DRAFT scene on the wall. The plan records that the store does not enforce one draft. So if a second draft ever exists (written over MCP later, or by anything other than draft_scene), walls would stage works from a draft no screen shows. Either stage only the draft that draft_scene/scene_draft would return, through one shared helper, or state in _control_layer that every draft stages on purpose.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "DisplayService absorbs a ninth concern; scenes could be a collaborator behind _control_layer", "recommendation": "Class, for later. display.py is now 2548 lines. Scenes add about 250 more: ten methods, two module constants and two dataclasses. The coupling between scenes and the feed writer is narrow: _control_layer(wall_id) going in, and _patch_walls going out. That makes scenes a clean candidate for their own module (e.g. programming/scenes.py) that _publish_feed calls. Not worth doing mid-wave. Worth a backlog line before 02 adds the HTTP and MCP surface on top of these methods.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "The heartbeat's new expiry step sits outside the bulkhead that protects the horizon roll", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `record_heartbeat` now runs `with self._store.transaction(): self._settle_scenes()` between `_record_screen` and `_roll_v2`, with no guard. `keep_showing`'s bulkhead comment states the rule that applies: 'Whatever fails here, the heartbeat's later steps still run, and one of them rolls the wall's horizon forward.' If the store write in `_settle_scenes` fails (the comment names a held lock or a full disk), the heartbeat is refused after its file is already written, and that wall's roll is skipped. For a persistent fault this happens on every heartbeat. Expiry bookkeeping is the store's account of something the Player already did, so it should never cost a roll. Wrap it like `keep_showing` (log with `event` and `wall_id`, then continue), and add a test that a failing `update_scene` still rolls. This cites core.md: 'When code is added, moved or retired, ask which guards were scoped to the old shape...'.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Unpin, discard and draft change wall feeds with no log line; pin logs without an event", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `unpin_scene` and `discard_scene` rewrite each affected wall's feed to drop staging, and `_patch_v2_works`/`_publish_feed` log nothing. These acts therefore leave no trace in the journal. `pin_scene` logs prose with no `extra` event, while apply, release and expire each carry `scene.*` events naming the walls. When someone asks why a Frame uploaded or evicted a work, the journal answers for applied scenes and is silent about staging. Give pin, unpin and discard one structured event each (`scene.staged`, `scene.unstaged`, `scene.discarded`, with `scene_id` and `wall_ids`), in the shape the other three use.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "oid": "O-11"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Ended scenes and their pins accumulate with no recorded lifecycle", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Discarded drafts are deleted, but every applied scene stays in `scenes` and `scene_pins` forever as `ended` ('Kept so History can say when'). 02 moves the History events into `history_events`, so that reason for keeping the rows is about to go. `list_scenes(status)` filters an unindexed `status` column. `_control_layer` calls it twice per feed write per wall, and `_settle_scenes` calls it on every heartbeat, so that work grows with every scene ever applied. At household scale the cost is small. What is missing is the bound (surface.md: 'When a result gains a collection, write down what bounds it before deciding it needs no cap'). In data-model.md's Scene entry, either state the retention (kept forever, and why, as #308 does for history_events), or prune ended scenes once 02's History events exist.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/persistence/sqlite.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "oid": "O-12"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Mid-build decisions for the owner (Goal 5 ledger)", "recommendation": "Scope: none. The plan records eight 01 decisions, each naming what settled it: (1) patch, not republish-keeping-slot, settled by the plan's resume goal; (2) `_publish_feed` as the single speller, settled by the plan's DECISION; (3) `Published.works` narrowed to the schedule's works plus `control_works`/`carried`, settled by 'do it right'. Its consumers are all in display.py and were re-read; (4) withdrawal, reconciliation and showings consider carried works, found while reading `exclude_work`; (5) `draft_scene` returns the existing draft; (6) History events move to 02, an alignment-pass miss the plan itself names; (7) a pin to a feedless wall is refused, which is still an inference awaiting the owner; (8) data-model marked built at 01. This makes the plan's data-model disposition ('03 marks the entry built') stale, and nothing acts on it. Downstream impact on the Player (the feed contract) is evidenced by the governed_by dispositions citing `what_to_show`, and by feed_guard validation. Items for the owner to veto: (7), plus the plan's still-inferred assumptions (no scene name, the 5/20/60 lengths, apply-ends-other, the showability rule, Skip/Show-now under a live scene, the History events).", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-wave-4f-scenes.md"], "oid": "O-13"}], "duration_seconds": 480, "scope": "wave-4f-scenes", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "chunk": null, "base_reviewed": null, "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-wave-4f-scenes.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md"], "chunk_graded": "01", "chunks_graded": ["01"], "plan_graded": ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-wave-4f-scenes.md", "findings": [], "unchecked": ["chunk-ref-missing graded chunk 01 of .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-wave-4f-scenes.md: chunk 01 was inferred as the plan's first open chunk (`plan_status.current_chunks`) because the dispatch carried no chunk \u2014 a chunk whose review is not yet recorded is still open, so this may be the next chunk rather than the reviewed one"], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": 0, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dead": 0, "change-fragment": 0, "retired-claim-survives": 0}}, "dispatched_at": "2026-10-26T18:37:20Z"}}
